WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.022
Half means two parts, and both
are exactly the same size.

2
00:00:08.242 --> 00:00:11.818
Cut a shape fairly, and each
part you get is one half.

3
00:00:12.318 --> 00:00:15.615
Look at this square. A line
goes straight down the middle.

4
00:00:15.835 --> 00:00:19.504
The two parts match. Swap them
over and you would not notice.

5
00:00:19.724 --> 00:00:24.368
That is equal. Equal means
exactly the same size, not nearly.

6
00:00:24.588 --> 00:00:28.025
Now look at this strip.
It is cut near one end.

7
00:00:28.245 --> 00:00:31.960
One part is fat and one is
thin. They are not equal.

8
00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.360
Two parts is not enough. Both
parts must be the same size.

9
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:40.064
This circle is already
cut into two equal parts.

10
00:00:40.284 --> 00:00:44.278
Colour one part, and then
stop. That piece is one half.

11
00:00:44.498 --> 00:00:48.631
One of two equal parts is
one half. One part, not both.

12
00:00:48.851 --> 00:00:52.427
Colour both parts and you
colour every bit of the circle.

13
00:00:52.647 --> 00:00:55.108
That is not a half.
That is the whole shape.

14
00:00:55.608 --> 00:00:59.184
Half is not only for shapes.
You can halve a pile of things.

15
00:00:59.404 --> 00:01:02.469
Here are six apples and
two plates. Do not guess.

16
00:01:02.689 --> 00:01:06.636
Deal them out one at a time,
like cards. Empty the pile.

17
00:01:06.856 --> 00:01:11.222
Three on each plate. Both plates
end up with exactly the same.

18
00:01:11.442 --> 00:01:14.692
So half of six is three,
and nobody is left out.

19
00:01:15.192 --> 00:01:18.490
Halving a number is the
same job as sharing a pile.

20
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:22.332
Take eight counters. Share
them into two equal rows.

21
00:01:22.552 --> 00:01:25.663
Now count one row only.
One, two, three, four.

22
00:01:25.883 --> 00:01:29.134
Four and four make eight,
so half of eight is four.

23
00:01:29.354 --> 00:01:32.698
Do not count them all. Eight
is what you started with.

24
00:01:32.918 --> 00:01:36.865
Halving makes a number
smaller. Half of ten is five.

25
00:01:37.365 --> 00:01:40.802
Here is one half of a circle.
It has a straight edge.

26
00:01:41.022 --> 00:01:44.458
Find the piece that fills
the empty space exactly.

27
00:01:44.678 --> 00:01:47.790
It has to be the same
size, and the same shape.

28
00:01:48.010 --> 00:01:51.354
Push the two halves together
and the circle is whole again.

29
00:01:51.574 --> 00:01:55.475
Every whole takes two halves.
Count them: two, four, six.

30
00:01:55.695 --> 00:01:58.899
Three whole oranges are
made from six halves.

31
00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:03.346
A half is one of two parts
that are exactly the same size.

32
00:02:03.566 --> 00:02:06.817
Half of a group, and half of
a number, is a fair share.

33
00:02:07.037 --> 00:02:10.567
And two halves always make one
whole. Now it is your turn.
